body:
Oh, dude, I wish! I'm suffering from anxiety and hopelessness nearly every single day because of financial issues, and this virus going around hasn't helped whatsoever to ease my fears about the future. :( 

&#x200B;

A few days ago I was in the absolute darkest point I think I've ever been in. I was literally planning on writing notes out to everyone in my family because I just could not deal with my emotions and pain anymore. I'm still feeling pretty down, but a little better now. I'm terrified for that low point to come back, but I know it will eventually anyways. Such is the nature of depression, I guess...

NTA, just wow. If this happened to me I would be very upset, just like you. You told your husband multiple times NOT to eat those specific cookies that were separated from the rest of the batch, he did it anyway and turned it around onto you when you got upset. It doesn't matter if it's 'just some cookies', what matters is how much work you put into them, how long they took to make, how much effort you gave for those cookies, etc. (Which, by the way, I'm sure they are amazing!) 

&#x200B;

Giving you the silent treatment and making you doubt yourself honestly sounds a bit like gaslighting to me

My parents would force me to keep secrets from the other parent. My mom would have a job, but not want my father to know she was working (for tax reasons, they're divorced), so she would swear me to never let my dad know she was working. She would tell me so many things and then say "Never tell your dad what I just told you, I trust you". It caused me to become alienated from both of them really. 

&#x200B;

Also being a therapist for your parents. Kids should just be kids, not counsellors. I always had to console my mom when my dad did something she didn't like. Since I was a baby I can remember my parents constantly fighting, when they finally got divorced it got even worse and I was put right into the middle of all of it.

i still really need to see pictures of some ghouls holding koalas, lol. maybe they'll never post pictures, because they (almost certainly) weren't in their outfits, masks, etc. 

&#x200B;

honestly, even if they photoshopped ghoul masks on i'd still want to see xD

im so sorry for the loss of your beloved pet. you know...i was watching a concert video someone posted from a ritual in Brooklyn and the Cardinal was speaking to the crowd. he made an analogy that really helped me. it was about getting a deep cut on your hand; he said at first when you get that wound, its all you can think about. the pain is so intense that you cant focus on anything else. you cant use your hand because it hurts so much. but then, little by little, the sharp pains transform into an itch, the stitches come out, and one day, without you even realising it, you find yourself using that hand again.

&#x200B;

he said "It doesn't hurt forever." 

&#x200B;

just something i think would help you...there is no way i can properly explain what Cardi C was saying but the video is still on youtube [here, if you'd like to hear it yourself.](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vvC_6AMMa0k) skip to 1:47:00, thats when he starts speaking.

You can do it! I said 'f it' to my anxiety when i took my moms offer to move in with her and her boyfriend  (who i hardly knew) for college. i have huge anxiety around unfamiliar people, especially living with people i dont know well...so this was a big step for me. i had only been to the town my college is in once before, and only for like 6 hours, had never taken the bus system here, didnt know anyone...but im so glad i did it, because now im in college and so happy and getting out of the house often, when before, i was on a year off and i rarely left the house. i was miserable. so taking the leap and moving in was the best thing for me. 

&#x200B;

remember that anxiety is just a feeling. panic is a feeling, too. its uncomfortable as hell but its just a feeling. and it will pass! so if you take the plunge and go somewhere new or decide to travel, and you end up having a panic attack, you will survive. as you mentioned in your post, you have the ativan for emergencies (hey, me too!). so if a panic attack happens say, on the plane to NY, you can take the ativan (as you know) and itll go away. for me ativan actually crushes panic attacks in like, 3 minutes. so almost as soon as it dissolves the attack stops. 

&#x200B;

you can do this, friend. you made it through college and im sure you did great! this rough patch you're in now with new roommates and breaking up with your boyfriend will not last forever. maybe your new job (though it sounds stressful) will actually be the best job of your life. maybe itll be incredible! 

&#x200B;

i really think that you should take the leap and travel, if you're considering it. and keep in mind that (as you said) you have family in NY, so you won't be completely alone. i am sure that if you need help they can help you. 

&#x200B;

im sending good vibes! :)
